const articleHtml = "<p>A laboratory website says its bird DNA test is 99% accurate. The number sits beside a photograph of a parrot, a certificate template, and a turnaround time. Nothing on the page says what was measured, against what reference, in how many birds, or what happened to samples that produced no result.</p>\n<p>That omission is not small. In avian testing, \"accuracy\" summarizes several distinct claims, each established by a different experiment. A laboratory can be excellent on one and mediocre on another, and a composite figure hides which is which. For a breeder or a veterinarian, that difference separates evidence from reassurance.</p>\n<p>This article explains how validation works, what a defensible reference panel looks like when the gold standard is hard to obtain, and why one percentage cannot carry the weight buyers place on it. The <a href=\"/bird-dna-testing-methods-accuracy/\">bird DNA testing methods and accuracy overview</a> places these concepts in the wider workflow.</p>\n<h2>\"Accuracy\" Is a Summary Word, Not a Measurement</h2>\n<p>Metrology separates accuracy into trueness — how close the average result sits to the true value — and precision — how tightly repeated results cluster. A method can be precise and wrong, consistently reporting the same incorrect sex, or accurate on average and too variable to trust for one bird.</p>\n<p>Qualitative assays, which most sexing and pathogen tests are, add another layer. There is no continuous value to compare; there is a classification. Validation asks whether the classification agrees with an independent determination of truth, and how often. That agreement needs an uncertainty estimate, because a study of forty birds and one of four thousand produce the same percentage with very different evidential weight.</p>\n<p>The word also travels badly between applications. A sexing assay's accuracy is agreement with the bird's genetic sex. A pathogen assay's accuracy is agreement with the presence of an organism, which may itself be defined by another test. A parentage assay's accuracy is a probability assigned to a relationship. Lumping these into one figure is where a claim first loses meaning. The <a href=\"/avian-molecular-biology-guide-the-foundation-of-bird-dna-testing/\">molecular biology primer</a> covers the assay layers beneath these distinctions.</p>\n<h2>Analytical Sensitivity and the Limit of Detection</h2>\n<p>Analytical sensitivity is the smallest amount of target an assay can reliably detect, expressed as a concentration or copy number and established by replicate dilutions.</p>\n<p>The limit of detection is usually the lowest concentration detected in 95% of replicate reactions. That takes more than one dilution series. CLSI EP17 sets out the design and separates LOD from the limit of blank — the highest signal from a sample with no target — and the limit of quantification.</p>\n<p>Three points get lost. LOD is matrix-dependent: feather extract, cloacal swab, and blood spot behave differently, so an LOD in buffer may be optimistic for field samples. LOD is assay-dependent, not platform-dependent: two qPCR assays on one instrument can differ by two orders of magnitude. And LOD is not diagnostic sensitivity: an assay detecting ten copies in a clean series can still miss infected birds.</p>\n<p>For sexing, LOD has a narrower role. The question is whether the W target is detected in a female, not how few copies can be seen. A low LOD helps with degraded feathers, but it is not proof of sexing accuracy.</p>\n<h2>Diagnostic Sensitivity and Specificity</h2>\n<p>Diagnostic sensitivity is the proportion of truly positive samples classified positive. Diagnostic specificity is the proportion of truly negative samples classified negative. Both need a reference standard independent of the assay under evaluation.</p>\n<p>For sexing, the reference is the bird's actual genetic sex. Obtaining it independently is harder than it sounds. Breeding records can establish sex when a pair produces fertile eggs. Gonadal inspection at necropsy is direct, but available only for deceased birds. A different genetic marker can serve as reference only if genuinely independent; a second CHD primer pair shares the same assumptions and can share the same failure. The <a href=\"/how-do-we-test-the-birds-gender-using-rt-pcr-method/\">RT-PCR sexing workflow</a> shows where those assumptions enter; the <a href=\"/the-complete-guide-to-bird-dna-sexing/\">complete sexing guide</a> covers sample choice.</p>\n<p>For pathogen testing, the reference may be culture, sequencing, or a composite of clinical signs and several assays. Each has limits. Culture can miss viable-but-non-culturable organisms; sequencing confirms what was amplified, not that the bird carried it; clinical signs are neither sensitive nor specific. A report should name its reference and its imperfections.</p>\n<p>Two-by-two tables also hide prevalence. Sensitivity and specificity belong to the assay, but the predictive value of a positive depends on how common the condition is. A pathogen assay with 99% specificity applied where 1% of birds are infected produces roughly as many false positives as true positives. A sexing assay in a half-female population faces friendlier arithmetic.</p>\n<h2>Repeatability, Reproducibility, and the Conditions Between</h2>\n<p>Repeatability is agreement between repeated measurements of one sample under the same conditions: same laboratory, operator, instrument, short interval. It captures noise inside a run and the stability of one extraction.</p>\n<p>Reproducibility is agreement under changed conditions: different operators, reagent lots, instruments, days, or laboratories. It captures the variation a customer experiences across a testing program.</p>\n<p>Between them sits intermediate precision: variation within one laboratory across days, runs, and operators. CLSI EP15 describes how a laboratory verifies precision; ISO 5725 sets the wider framework. A laboratory can look excellent on repeatability and fail on reproducibility: one technician running triplicates produces tight agreement, while three technicians across six weeks and two lot changes may not.</p>\n<p>For a qualitative assay, agreement is usually summarized by percent concordance or Cohen's kappa. Concordance misleads when one category dominates; kappa corrects for chance agreement, and reporting both is easier to interpret.</p>\n<h2>Designing the Reference Panel</h2>\n<p>The panel is the foundation of the study. If it is unrepresentative, every downstream statistic inherits the bias.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Reference type</th>\n<th>Strength</th>\n<th>Limitation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Breeding records with fertile eggs</td>\n<td>Independent of the assay; real birds</td>\n<td>Retrospective; record quality; adults only</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gonadal inspection at necropsy</td>\n<td>Direct anatomical evidence</td>\n<td>Requires deceased birds</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Independent genetic marker</td>\n<td>Works on live birds; can be blinded</td>\n<td>May share assumptions with the test</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Culture or sequencing</td>\n<td>Direct organism evidence</td>\n<td>Culture sensitivity varies; DNA is not viability</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The panel should include the sample types the laboratory actually receives, not the easiest ones to validate. A sexing study built on fresh blood says little about shed feathers; a pathogen study built on high-titer isolates says little about low-level swabs. The panel should span good, marginal, and rejectable samples. The <a href=\"/bird-species-dna-testing/\">bird species testing overview</a> and the <a href=\"/test-bird-dna-at-home-what-equipment-and-reagents-do-you-need/\">home sampling equipment guide</a> show how widely collection conditions vary; the <a href=\"/sample-shipping/\">shipping guidance</a> covers the gap between collection and extraction.</p>\n<p>Blinding matters too. Whoever reads the gel or curve should not know the reference result, or the reading drifts toward expectation. Randomizing order and duplicating samples under different identifiers catches labeling errors.</p>\n<h2>Sample Size and the 99% Illusion</h2>\n<p>A percentage without an interval is an incomplete statement. The table shows what 99% agreement implies at different study sizes, using a 95% Wilson interval for the lower bound.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Validation result</th>\n<th>Reported agreement</th>\n<th>95% lower bound</th>\n<th>What it supports</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>49 of 50 correct</td>\n<td>98%</td>\n<td>about 89.5%</td>\n<td>Screening-level evidence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>99 of 100 correct</td>\n<td>99%</td>\n<td>about 94.6%</td>\n<td>Reasonable but imprecise</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>198 of 200 correct</td>\n<td>99%</td>\n<td>about 96.4%</td>\n<td>Stronger, still scope-limited</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>297 of 300 correct</td>\n<td>99%</td>\n<td>about 97.1%</td>\n<td>Good precision for tested scope</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>990 of 1,000 correct</td>\n<td>99%</td>\n<td>about 98.2%</td>\n<td>Strong evidence in that population</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>A related trap is the perfect score. If a laboratory reports 100% agreement on 30 samples, the observed failure rate is zero, but the 95% upper bound is about 9.5% by the rule of three. The study is compatible with true accuracy near 90.5%, so \"100% accurate\" is arithmetically true and evidentially misleading.</p>\n<p>There is a third trap: the denominator. Suppose a laboratory reports 99% concordance among samples that produced a result, but 15% were inconclusive and excluded. The figure describes the easier 85%, not the service a customer receives. A report should state the inconclusive rate and explain how those samples were handled.</p>\n<h2>Verification, Then Monitoring</h2>\n<p>A laboratory adopting a published assay — say, a CHD primer pair — is not automatically validated for its own use. It must verify that it can reproduce published performance with its own extraction, thermocyclers, and sample types. Verification is narrower: the intended use is established, and the laboratory confirms its implementation meets it.</p>\n<p>Verification typically covers known positives and negatives, expected fragment sizes or Ct ranges, a contamination check, and a comparison against published criteria. Change the primers, extraction chemistry, instrument, or sample type, and verification repeats for the affected scope.</p>\n<p>After launch, monitoring takes over. Three practices separate a maintained assay from a validated-then-forgotten one: batch controls in every run, including positive, negative, extraction blanks, and an internal amplification control; proficiency testing with blinded external samples; and trend monitoring of inconclusive rates, repeats, control failures, and turnaround.</p>\n<p>Documentation closes the loop. A validation report should identify the assay version, species and sample types, the reference panel and how truth was established, results by stratum, confidence intervals, LOD, precision estimates, pre-specified acceptance criteria, and deviations. Without pre-specified criteria, a laboratory can always describe results as acceptable afterward. The <a href=\"/what-is-an-avian-dna-lab-what-does-it-do/\">avian laboratory overview</a> describes the quality system these documents sit inside.</p>\n<h2>Reading a Validation Claim: Seven Questions</h2>\n<p>When a laboratory presents an accuracy figure, these questions turn a slogan into evidence.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Which species and sample types were tested?</strong> A claim covering \"birds\" does not cover your bird.</li>\n<li><strong>How was reference truth established?</strong> Breeding records, necropsy, culture, sequencing, or another assay, each with different weight.</li>\n<li><strong>How many samples, and how many of each sex or condition?</strong> A skewed panel can hide a one-sided failure.</li>\n<li><strong>What is the confidence interval?</strong> Without one, the point estimate does too much work.</li>\n<li><strong>What was the inconclusive rate, and how were those samples counted?</strong> The denominator changes the meaning.</li>\n<li><strong>What is the LOD, and in which matrix?</strong> Relevant for pathogens and low-DNA samples; less so for routine sexing.</li>\n<li><strong>What happens when a result is ambiguous?</strong> A defined repeat or confirmation pathway signals a working quality system.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>A laboratory that answers all seven is not automatically better than one that answers five. The answers show whether the number came from a study or a template. The site's <a href=\"/faq/\">FAQ</a> covers related questions.</p>\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>\n<h3>What accuracy can a bird DNA test claim?</h3>\n<p>There is no universal figure. Accuracy depends on the assay, species, sample type, reference standard, and the share of samples that produce a call. A laboratory should report performance for a defined scope, with a sample size and confidence interval.</p>\n<h3>What is the difference between analytical and diagnostic sensitivity?</h3>\n<p>Analytical sensitivity is the smallest amount of target an assay can detect, usually a limit of detection. Diagnostic sensitivity is the proportion of truly positive birds the assay classifies positive. The first belongs to the chemistry; the second applies the test to a population.</p>\n<h3>Why does a 99% accurate test still produce wrong results?</h3>\n<p>Because 99% is an average over a panel, and any sample can fall in the 1%. Errors cluster around contamination, sample mix-up, inhibited reactions, genetic variants affecting primer binding, and ambiguous band interpretation. High concordance lowers the probability of error; it does not remove the need for controls.</p>\n<h3>How many birds should a validation study include?</h3>\n<p>Enough to produce an interval narrow enough for the claim. A few dozen samples support a screening claim; a claim of 99% or better needs several hundred, stratified by species and sex. The number depends on the precision required and should be justified in the protocol.</p>\n<h3>What is the difference between repeatability and reproducibility?</h3>\n<p>Repeatability is agreement under identical conditions: same operator, instrument, and run. Reproducibility is agreement when conditions change, across days, reagent lots, operators, or laboratories. A method can be repeatable without being reproducible, which is why validation varies conditions.</p>\n<h3>Can one primer set be validated for all bird species?</h3>\n<p>No. CHD intron lengths and primer-binding sites vary across taxa, so a pair that works in one group may give indistinguishable fragments or fail in another. Validation is species- or clade-specific, and a laboratory should name the species it verified.</p>\n<h3>Is accreditation the same as assay validation?</h3>\n<p>No. Accreditation confirms a quality management system and competence for defined activities. Validation confirms that a specific assay performs as intended for a specific use. An accredited laboratory still has to validate a new species or sample type.</p>\n<h2>The Author's Take</h2>\n<p><strong>Position.</strong> A validation claim is only as strong as its reference panel and its denominator. The percentage is the least informative part of the report.</p>\n<p><strong>Reasoning.</strong> Two laboratories can both say 99% and mean different things: one tested 990 birds across six species with necropsy-confirmed sex and reported a 2% inconclusive rate; the other tested 50 blood samples from one species, excluded the awkward cases, and compared against the same primer set it was validating. The first supports a procurement decision; the second supports a brochure. Buyers rarely see which one they are reading.</p>\n<p><strong>Disclosure.</strong> This is the author's editorial judgment, grounded in published validation guidance and laboratory practice. It is not a certification, an audit finding, or a guarantee of any individual test result.</p>\n<h2>Sources</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Clinical and Laboratory Standards Institute. (2012). <em>EP17-A2: Evaluation of Detection Capability for Clinical Laboratory Measurement Procedures.</em> <a href=\"https://clsi.org/standards/products/method-evaluation/documents/ep17/\">CLSI EP17</a></li>\n<li>Clinical and Laboratory Standards Institute. <em>EP15: User Verification of Precision and Estimation of Bias.</em> <a href=\"https://clsi.org/standards/products/method-evaluation/documents/ep15/\">CLSI EP15</a></li>\n<li>Bustin, S. A., Benes, V., Garson, J. A., et al. (2009). The MIQE guidelines: minimum information for publication of quantitative real-time PCR experiments. <em>Clinical Chemistry</em>, 55(4), 611–622. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1373/clinchem.2008.112797\">doi:10.1373/clinchem.2008.112797</a></li>\n<li>Forootan, A., Sjöback, R., Björkman, J., Sjögreen, B., Linz, L., &amp; Kubista, M. (2017). Methods to determine limit of detection and limit of quantification in quantitative real-time PCR (qPCR). <em>Biomolecular Detection and Quantification</em>, 12, 1–6. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bdq.2017.04.001\">doi:10.1016/j.bdq.2017.04.001</a></li>\n<li>Casey, A. E., Jones, K. L., Sandercock, B. K., &amp; Wisely, S. M. (2009). Heteroduplex molecules cause sexing errors in a standard molecular protocol for avian sexing. <em>Molecular Ecology Resources</em>, 9(1), 61–65. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1755-0998.2008.02307.x\">doi:10.1111/j.1755-0998.2008.02307.x</a></li>\n<li>Morinha, F., Cabral, J. A., &amp; Bastos, E. (2012). Molecular sexing of birds: a comparative review of PCR-based methods. <em>Theriogenology</em>, 78(4), 703–714. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.theriogenology.2012.04.015\">doi:10.1016/j.theriogenology.2012.04.015</a></li>\n<li>Kralik, P., &amp; Ricchi, M. (2017). A basic guide to real time PCR in microbial diagnostics. <em>Frontiers in Microbiology</em>, 8, 108. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3389/fmicb.2017.00108\">doi:10.3389/fmicb.2017.00108</a></li>\n</ol>";
